# ===========================================================================
# Signal channel for the agents. OPTIONAL add-on -- nothing in the base
# build reads this file.
#
# Copied to config/signal.env (0600). Never commit the filled-in copy, and
# never paste a value from it into a chat with an agent.
#
# Unlike Telegram, Signal has no bot API and no free second identity: an
# account IS a phone number. That single fact drives everything below, and
# it is why this file asks you for a number rather than a token.
#
# You fill in the top section. The bottom section is written for you by
#
#     ./scripts/addon-signal.sh link
#
# which links signal-cli to a Signal account and records what it linked.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Which agent gets Signal ------------------------------------------------
# One of: hermes | openclaw | both
#
# "both" needs TWO phone numbers, and the reason is not a limitation of this
# repo. Every device linked to a Signal account receives every message sent
# to it, so two agents on one account both answer the same message -- the
# user sees doubled replies and the agents may answer each other. There is
# no per-device addressing in Signal to divide them with.
#
# Start with one. Adding the second later is re-running this add-on with a
# second number; nothing is wasted.
SIGNAL_AGENT="hermes"

# --- How you want to reach the agent ----------------------------------------
# One of: note-to-self | dedicated
#
#   note-to-self   Link signal-cli to YOUR OWN Signal account, and talk to
#                  the agent by messaging yourself -- Signal's "Note to
#                  Self" conversation. Costs nothing: no second number, no
#                  captcha, no new SIM. One QR scan and you are done.
#
#                  HERMES ONLY. It is a documented Hermes feature: the
#                  adapter picks up your Note-to-Self messages and replies
#                  in the same thread, with echo-protection so it does not
#                  answer itself. OpenClaw has no equivalent and explicitly
#                  ignores your own messages, so it cannot work this way.
#
#                  The trade-off: the agent shares your Signal identity, so
#                  its replies are sent AS YOU and appear in your Note to
#                  Self thread on every device you own. If you use that
#                  thread as a scratchpad, you will notice.
#
#   dedicated      The agent gets its own number and its own identity. You
#                  message it like any other contact. Required for OpenClaw,
#                  and required if you want both agents on Signal.
#
# Start with note-to-self unless you have a reason not to. Switching later
# costs one unlink and one re-link.
SIGNAL_MODE="note-to-self"

# --- The number signal-cli links to (E.164, e.g. +15551234567) --------------
# What goes here depends on SIGNAL_MODE above.
#
#   note-to-self   YOUR OWN number -- the one Signal already runs on your
#                  phone. Hermes requires SIGNAL_ACCOUNT to match your
#                  number for Note to Self to work at all.
#
#   dedicated      The AGENT's number, not yours. It must already have
#                  Signal running on some phone, because this add-on LINKS
#                  (attaches as a secondary device, the way Signal Desktop
#                  does) rather than registering. In practice: an old
#                  handset, a cheap prepaid SIM, or a second number on a
#                  dual-SIM phone.
#
# Why link rather than register: registering a number directly with
# signal-cli needs a captcha solved from the same public IP as the VM --
# which has no browser and leaves through a proxy -- and it DE-AUTHENTICATES
# the Signal app on that number. Linking needs neither.
SIGNAL_HERMES_NUMBER=""
SIGNAL_OPENCLAW_NUMBER=""

# --- Who may message the agents ---------------------------------------------
# YOUR OWN Signal number, in E.164. Comma-separate for more than one.
# In note-to-self mode this is the same number as above, and that is correct
# -- you are the only sender, and the allowlist still has to say so.
#
# This is the whole access control, exactly as TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USER_ID is
# for Telegram. A live agent has shell access and no permission prompts, so
# whoever may message it can run commands on it. Blank is refused: this
# add-on will not create a reachable agent that answers anybody.
#
# Note that Signal identifies senders by phone number OR by UUID. A sender
# whose number is hidden arrives as a UUID and will NOT match a number in
# this list -- they are refused, which is the safe direction, but it is why
# a correct-looking entry can still leave you unable to talk to your agent.
# verify.sh reports which form was recorded.
SIGNAL_ALLOWED_USERS=""

# --- Written by `addon-signal.sh link` --------------------------------------
# LEAVE BLANK. Recorded after linking so the scripts can tell a finished
# link from an unfinished one, and so verify.sh can check the right account.
# No key material is stored here -- the account keys live only inside the
# VM, under the signal-cli data directory.
SIGNAL_HERMES_LINKED=""
SIGNAL_OPENCLAW_LINKED=""
